## Part 4: Throughput Paths — All Variants, All Paths

### 4.1 Wireless Paths (On-Phone Proximity Advantage)

| Variant | Radio | Band | Real Sustained | Burst | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Single C5 (any form/storage) | 1× ESP32-C5 | 5 GHz Wi-Fi 6 | **6–9 MB/s** | ~10 MB/s | Proximity = high SNR = top modulation |
| Dual C5 (any form/storage) | 2× ESP32-C5, SPI AGG | 5 GHz + 2.4 GHz agg. | **12–15 MB/s** | ~16–18 MB/s | SPI link prevents inter-chip bottleneck. RF isolation critical. |
| Single E22-MINI (roadmap) | 1× ESP32-E22 | 6 GHz Wi-Fi 6E | **~20–40 MB/s** (projected) | Higher | Requires larger battery + thermal mgmt. |
| Dual E22-MINI (roadmap) | 2× ESP32-E22, SPI AGG | Tri-band agg. | **~40–70 MB/s** (projected) | Higher | Extreme tier. Significant thermal/power. |

### 4.2 Wired Paths (USB Bridge Bypass)

| Bridge | USB Speed | Real Sustained | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| **GL3224** (Primary) | USB 3.0 (5 Gbps) | **70–100+ MB/s** | Bridge owns storage when plugged in; bypasses radio entirely. Computer sees USB mass storage. |
| PL2732 (Backup 1) | USB 3.0 (5 Gbps) | **70–100+ MB/s** | Equivalent performance. |
| GL823 (Backup 2) | USB 2.0 (480 Mbps) | **25–35 MB/s** | Smallest/cheapest. Still fast for most users. |
| ESP32 native USB | Full-Speed (12 Mbps) | **~0.5 MB/s** | **Never used for data.** Only for flashing/JTAG. |

### 4.3 Trickle Backup (The Winning Lever)

Trickle sync (automatic background backup when near and idle) makes raw throughput less critical for daily use. The big transfer never has to happen if trickle is active. Strong on Android; partial on iOS (OS background limits).

